Look at the Pixel 5 screen in your photo: AndroidWifi still says "Limited connection" (and the Wi-Fi icon has the ! mark), AND notice the "Em... isn't responding" dialog peeking out behind it because the emulator is lagging slightly.
Even though you installed cacert.cer, Android's built-in connectivity check (://gstatic.com) ignores User-installed certificates on modern Android versions—which is why AndroidWifi stays stuck on "Limited connection" and refuses to route internet traffic properly!
Follow these two steps to fix the "Limited connection" error permanently:

1. Disable Android's Captive Portal Check via adb (Fixes "Limited connection" Immediately)

Open your Ubuntu terminal and run these two commands to stop Android from marking AndroidWifi as "Limited connection" when routed through Burp Suite:
bash
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb shell settings put global captive_portal_mode 0
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb shell settings put global captive_portal_detection_enabled 0
Use code with caution.
• Toggle Wi-Fi off and on. On the Pixel 5 screen shown in your photo, tap Disconnect (the X icon), wait 3 seconds, and tap Connect (or turn Airplane mode on and off).
• Check the Wi-Fi icon. The ! exclamation mark will disappear immediately, and AndroidWifi will show Connected with a solid white Wi-Fi icon!

2. Move Burp's Certificate to the System Store (So Apps Trust It)

Because Android apps (and Android's system services) ignore User certificates, install Burp's certificate into the System trust store so your app doesn't reject Burp's HTTPS connection:
1. Export Burp's certificate to your Ubuntu Desktop:
	• In Burp Suite (shown on the right side of your screen), go to Proxy > Proxy settings, click Import / export CA certificate, select Certificate in DER format, click Next, and save it as cacert.der on your Desktop.
2. Convert it to Android's hashed format in your Ubuntu terminal:bash
openssl x509 -inform DER -in ~/Desktop/cacert.der -out ~/Desktop/cacert.pem
HASH=$(openssl x509 -inform PEM -subject_hash_old -in ~/Desktop/cacert.pem | head -1)
cp ~/Desktop/cacert.pem ~/Desktop/${HASH}.0
Use code with caution.
3. Push it to Android's System certificates folder:
	• Close the emulator (killall -9 qemu-system-x86_64) and relaunch it with -writable-system:bash
myphone -writable-system -no-snapshot-load -http-proxy http://127.0.0.1:8080
Use code with caution.
	• Once it boots, open a new terminal tab and run:bash
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb root
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb remount
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb push ~/Desktop/${HASH}.0 /system/etc/security/cacerts/
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb shell chmod 644 /system/etc/security/cacerts/${HASH}.0
~/Android/Sdk/platform-tools/adb reboot
Use code with caution.
Once the emulator reboots, AndroidWifi will stay Connected (solid white icon), and your app will trust Burp Suite's SSL certificate cleanly!
